# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LOCAL VERSION OVERRIDES - an OPTIONAL, NORMALLY EMPTY file.
#
# WHAT USED TO BE HERE, AND WHY IT MOVED. This file held every gateway's pinned image / repo /
# commit, with the rationale for each pin. That made it a shared file naming every gateway in the
# field, and it broke the one structural invariant this repo has:
#
#     Adding a gateway is exactly: drop in gateways/<name>/gateway.sh, and it works.
#
# It was not true. A gateway that built from source ALSO needed a block added here, in a file it did
# not own, or its build had no ref at all. Every pin now lives in the gateway's own manifest, next to
# that manifest's own disclosure of what it deviates from and why:
#
#     gateways/<name>/gateway.sh    ->   <NAME>_IMAGE / <NAME>_REPO / <NAME>_COMMIT, each written as
#                                        VAR="${VAR:-<pinned value>}"
#
# NOTHING WAS LOOSENED. The pins are the same values, still exact, still recorded: the runner stamps
# the version/commit ACTUALLY built into every run JSON (`build` field), so the output still discloses
# what was measured, and "you chose an old branch" is still answerable by pointing at the manifest and
# at the recorded commit. What changed is only WHERE the pin lives, which is now the same place as
# every other fact about that gateway.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR NOW. The runners still `source` it before each gateway manifest, and every
# manifest pin is written as `VAR="${VAR:-default}"`, so anything assigned here still WINS. That makes
# this the seam for a LOCAL, UNCOMMITTED override, e.g. benchmarking a locally built image instead of
# the released one, or trying a newer upstream commit without editing a manifest:
#
#     SOMEGATEWAY_IMAGE=somegateway:dev
#     SOMEGATEWAY_COMMIT=
#
# Committing an override here re-couples a shared file to one gateway. If a pin should CHANGE, change
# it in that gateway's own manifest, in a commit that says why. lib/gateway_isolation_test.sh enforces
# that: it fails if any gateway's name appears in this file.
#
# NOTE ON SCOPE. Envoy AI Gateway is intentionally NOT in this benchmark: it is Kubernetes-native
# (Envoy Gateway + CRDs, a full cluster), not a single-box drop-in, which is out of scope for this
# harness. That is a statement about a project with no directory here, so it names nothing in the field.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
